  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Provision of Onward Transport and Cash Assistance to Returnees affected by Sudan Crisis</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since April 2023, when violence broke out in Sudan, over 135,000 people have crossed into South Sudan as of 25 June. This number could double by October 2023. The situation in Sudan has affected the economy and security of South Sudan, especially in the northern states that depend on trade with Sudan. Protection monitoring conducted at the border indicates high numbers of vulnerable arrivals, including unaccompanied or separated children, older persons, persons with disabilities, those with urgent medical needs, single- or female-headed households, and pregnant women. An estimated 17,068 returnees are in collective centers whereas 24,206 returnees have been integrated into host communities. The International Organization for Migration (IOM) alone has facilitated over 35,000 returnees to key locations, with 72,000 more people estimated to be in need of such support in the next six months. 
 
In response, IOM is providing life-saving support to the most vulnerable new arrivals in South Sudan who face protection, health and physical risks. In coordination with the Government of South Sudan and other humanitarian actors, IOM is providing onward transportation assistance to 15,000 returnees through various modes of transportation, safeguarding dignified and safe human mobility and physical wellbeing of returnees.</narrative>
  </description>
